      "prompt": "Why do the compounds hexane, hexanol, and hexene have such similar names?",
      "answer": {
        "type": "open",
        "generated_by": "ai",
        "solution": "Suggested approach: take each name apart. The stem <em>hex-</em> is the same in all three because each comes from the same chain of six carbon atoms; the ending is what tells the families apart, <em>-ane</em> for the alkane with only single bonds, <em>-ene</em> for the alkene with a carbon–carbon double bond, and <em>-ol</em> for the alcohol with an –OH group in place of a hydrogen atom."
